sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/fisher-kpp-speed> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis>;
    dc:description "An advantageous allele spreading through a continuous population advances as a travelling wave whose speed is set by the selection coefficient and the diffusion (dispersal) rate.";
    dc:source "Ronald A. Fisher (1937) The wave of advance of advantageous genes";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Fisher-KPP wave of advance of an advantageous gene";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(78)90130-5>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "u_t = D u_{xx} + s u(1-u); travelling waves u(x-ct) exist iff c \\ge c^* = 2\\sqrt{sD}; compactly supported initial data spread at asymptotic speed c^*";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: one spatial dimension (extended to R^n), constant diffusion D, logistic (genic) selection with coefficient s, deterministic large-population limit. Formal status: proved; Kolmogorov, Petrovsky and Piskunov (1937) gave the proof independently, and Aronson and Weinberger (1978) proved the asymptotic spreading speed in several dimensions. Finite populations, Allee effects or long-tailed dispersal change the speed.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Measure the front speed of a beneficial allele, or of an expanding population, in an experimental range expansion (e.g. bacteria on agar or laboratory insect arenas) with independently measured s and D, and compare it with 2*sqrt(sD)."
}
